{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L1.001","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":"1_obvious","formula_form":"named_directly"},"prompt":"State the Pythagorean theorem for a right triangle with legs of length a and b and hypotenuse of length c. Express c² as an algebraic expression in a and b. Output only the right-hand side of the equation c² = ____, using ^ for exponentiation (e.g. write 'x^2 + y^2', NOT 'xy').","ground_truth":"a^2 + b^2","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["The response is a valid mathematical expression equivalent to a^2 + b^2.","The response contains only the requested expression, apart from harmless surrounding whitespace or math delimiters."],"fail_criteria":["The submitted expression is undefined, malformed, or not mathematically equivalent to the target.","The response changes the original domain or introduces an invalid extra solution/restriction."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["geometry"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L1.002","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":"1_obvious","formula_form":"named_directly"},"prompt":"State Newton's second law of motion as an equation: net force F equals mass m times what other quantity? Output only the symbol for that quantity (one letter).","ground_truth":"a","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"a","case_sensitive":false},"tags":["physics","mechanics"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L1.003","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":"1_obvious","formula_form":"named_directly"},"prompt":"Ohm's law for a resistor relates voltage V, current I, and resistance R. Express V as the algebraic product of two of these quantities. Output ONLY the right-hand side of V = ____ as a multiplication (e.g. 'XY' or 'X*Y'). Do NOT use division or addition.","ground_truth":"IR","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*\\(?\\s*(I\\s*[*·×]?\\s*R|R\\s*[*·×]?\\s*I)\\s*\\)?\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["physics","electricity"],"notes":"Regex enforces a pure product of I and R, rejecting I/R, I+R, etc."}
{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L1.004","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":"1_obvious","formula_form":"named_directly"},"prompt":"State the area A of a triangle with base b and height h using the standard half-base-times-height formula. Output ONLY the right-hand side of A = ____, with no units or explanatory text. Equivalent algebraic forms are accepted, provided the expression is exactly one half times b times h.","ground_truth":"(1/2)*b*h","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"(1/2)*b*h","case_sensitive":false,"strip_whitespace":true},"tags":["geometry"],"notes":"Regex requires the 1/2 factor along with both b and h in product form."}
{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L1.005","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":"1_obvious","formula_form":"named_directly"},"prompt":"State the area A of a circle with radius r. Output only the right-hand side of A = ____. Use pi or π for the constant, * for multiplication, and either ^2 or ² for the square (for example, pi*r^2 or π*r²).","ground_truth":"pi*r^2","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"\\A(?:(?:\\s*(?:pi|π)\\s*\\*?\\s*r(?:\\^2|²)\\s*)|(?:\\s*r(?:\\^2|²)\\s*\\*?\\s*(?:pi|π)\\s*)|(?:\\s*(?:pi|π)\\s*\\*\\s*r\\s*\\*\\s*r\\s*)|(?:\\s*r\\s*\\*\\s*r\\s*\\*\\s*(?:pi|π)\\s*))\\Z","flags":["i"]},"tags":["geometry"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L2.001","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":"not_explicit_law_name_recall","formula_form":"implicit_via_regime"},"prompt":"A monochromatic X-ray beam strikes a crystal whose atoms form parallel planes separated by distance d. Constructive interference of the reflected beam requires a specific relationship between the wavelength λ, the spacing d, the integer order n, and the glancing angle θ. What is the conventional NAME of this law? Output only the name (form: 'X's law' or just X).","ground_truth":"Bragg's law","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)\\A\\s*(?:bragg|bragg\\ law|bragg's\\ law)\\s*\\s*\\Z","flags":[]},"tags":["physics","crystallography"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L2.002","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":"2-3_plausible","formula_form":"implicit_via_regime"},"prompt":"You are given the three side lengths of a triangle and want to compute its area without knowing any altitude or angle. The classical formula uses the semi-perimeter and the three side lengths. What is the conventional NAME of this formula? Output only the name (form: 'X's formula' or just X).","ground_truth":"Heron's formula","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)\\A\\s*(?:Heron|Heron\\ formula|Heron's\\ formula)\\s*\\s*\\Z","flags":[]},"tags":["geometry"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L2.003","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":"2-3_plausible","formula_form":"implicit_via_regime"},"prompt":"In an infinitely large randomly mating diploid population with no selection, mutation, migration, or genetic drift, allele frequencies and genotype frequencies remain constant from generation to generation, with genotype frequencies p², 2pq, q² for alleles at frequencies p and q. What is the NAME of this principle? Output only the name (form: 'X principle' or 'X equilibrium' or 'X-Y').","ground_truth":"Hardy-Weinberg","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)\\A\\s*hardy(?:\\s|-)+weinberg(?:\\s+(?:principle|equilibrium))?\\s*\\Z","flags":[]},"tags":["biology","genetics"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id": "know.formula_recall.L2.004", "canonical_id": "know.formula_recall", "category": "know", "level": "L2", "axis_values": {"n_candidate_formulas": "2-3_plausible", "formula_form": "implicit_via_regime"}, "prompt": "For a polynomial with given coefficients, there is a classical set of identities expressing the elementary symmetric functions of its roots (their sum, pairwise-product sum, product, etc.) directly in terms of the coefficients. What is the conventional name of these identities? Output only the name.", "ground_truth": "Vieta's formulas", "scorer": {"type": "regex", "pattern": "(?i)^\\s*(?:vieta(?:['’]s)?\\s*(?:formulas|formulae|theorem)?|vi(?:e|è)te(?:['’]s)?\\s*(?:formulas|formulae|theorem)?|vieta|vi(?:e|è)te)\\s*$", "flags": []}, "tags": ["mathematics", "algebra"], "notes": ""}
{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L2.005","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":"2-3_plausible","formula_form":"implicit_via_regime"},"prompt":"You want to compute the cell potential of an electrochemical half-cell or full cell under nonstandard-state conditions, given the standard potential E°, temperature T, the number of electrons transferred n, and the reaction quotient Q. What is the NAME of the equation you would use? Output only the name (form: 'X equation' or just X).","ground_truth":"Nernst equation","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)\\A\\s*(?:Nernst|Nernst\\ equation)\\s*\\s*\\Z","flags":[]},"tags":["chemistry","electrochemistry"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L3.001","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":">3_competing","formula_form":"implicit_via_regime"},"prompt":"For a strictly massless particle (e.g. a photon in vacuum) with momentum magnitude p, the special-relativistic energy-momentum relation reduces to a particularly simple form. Express the total energy E as a single product of p and one fundamental constant. Output ONLY the right-hand side of E = ____ as that pure product (e.g. 'XY' or 'X*Y'). Do NOT include additional terms, exponents, or division.","ground_truth":"pc","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*\\(?\\s*(p\\s*[*·×]?\\s*c|c\\s*[*·×]?\\s*p)\\s*\\)?\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["physics","relativity"],"notes":"Regex enforces pure product of p and c, rejecting pc^2, p+c, p/c, p^2*c, etc."}
{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L3.002","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":">3_competing","formula_form":"implicit_via_regime"},"prompt":"In Compton scattering of an X-ray photon off a free electron initially at rest, the change in photon wavelength Δλ depends on the scattering angle θ, Planck's constant h, the electron rest mass m_e, and the speed of light c. Write the standard formula. Output ONLY the right-hand side of Δλ = ____ in plain text (use 'cos(theta)' for cosine, '*' for product, '/' for division, '^' for exponentiation). The answer MUST contain h, m_e (or m), c, and the factor (1 - cos(theta)).","ground_truth":"(h/(m_e*c))*(1 - cos(theta))","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"(h/(m_e*c))*(1 - cos(theta))","case_sensitive":false,"strip_whitespace":true},"tags":["physics","quantum"],"notes":"Lookahead regex requires h, m, c, and the literal '1 - cos' (or '1-cos') structure."}
{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L3.003","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":">3_competing","formula_form":"implicit_via_regime"},"prompt":"In general relativity, the Schwarzschild radius r_s of a non-rotating, uncharged black hole of mass M depends on Newton's gravitational constant G, M, and the speed of light c. Write the formula. Output ONLY the right-hand side of r_s = ____ in plain text (use '*' for product, '/' for division, '^' or '**' for exponentiation, e.g. 'c^2'). The numerator must equal 2*G*M and the denominator must equal c squared.","ground_truth":"2*G*M/c^2","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*\\(?\\s*2\\s*\\*\\s*G\\s*\\*\\s*M\\s*\\)?\\s*/\\s*\\(?\\s*c\\s*(?:\\^\\s*2|\\*\\*\\s*2|²)\\s*\\)?\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["physics","relativity"],"notes":"The accepted expression must preserve the exact 2*G*M/c^2 structure."}
{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L3.004","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":">3_competing","formula_form":"implicit_via_regime"},"prompt":"For a (possibly relativistic) particle with momentum magnitude p, the de Broglie wavelength λ is Planck's constant h divided by p. Write the formula. Output ONLY the right-hand side of λ = ____ exactly as h/p, using '/' for division. Do NOT include spaces, parentheses, additional terms, exponents, or extra symbols.","ground_truth":"h/p","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^h/p$","flags":[]},"tags":["physics","quantum"],"notes":"Regex enforces h/p exactly, rejecting hp, h*p, h+p, p/h, h/p^2, etc."}
{"probe_id":"know.formula_recall.L3.005","canonical_id":"know.formula_recall","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_candidate_formulas":">3_competing","formula_form":"implicit_via_regime"},"prompt":"In special relativity, the kinetic energy KE of a particle with rest mass m moving at speed v is NOT (1/2)mv². Instead, it is the total energy minus the rest energy. Using the Lorentz factor γ (gamma), write KE in terms of γ, m, and the speed of light c. Output ONLY the right-hand side of KE = ____ in plain text (use '*' for product, '^' or '**' for exponentiation; write 'gamma' or 'γ' for the Lorentz factor). The answer MUST contain (gamma - 1) (i.e. the Lorentz factor minus one) AND m AND c squared.","ground_truth":"(gamma - 1) * m * c^2","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"(gamma - 1) * m * c^2","case_sensitive":false,"strip_whitespace":true},"tags":["physics","relativity"],"notes":"Lookahead regex requires (gamma-1) factor, m, and c^2 (or c**2 or c²)."}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L1.001","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"headline_function","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"A muscle cell suddenly working hard runs short of oxygen and switches to producing lactic acid from pyruvate. Glycolysis can continue to make ATP only if a particular oxidized electron-carrier coenzyme is continuously available; the conversion of pyruvate to lactate replenishes its OXIDIZED form (not the reduced form). Name the OXIDIZED form of that coenzyme. Use the conventional one-token chemistry abbreviation (with a charge sign if appropriate). Output only that single token.","ground_truth":"NAD+","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*NAD(?:\\+|⁺)\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["biology","metabolism"],"notes":"Regex requires the '+' charge sign so 'NADH' (the reduced form, a wrong answer) does not match."}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L1.002","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"headline_function","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"Why does sweating cool the body? Identify the single physical process that removes heat from the skin as liquid sweat becomes water vapor. Output only one or two words naming that process.","ground_truth":"evaporation","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)\\A\\s*(?:evaporation|vaporization|evaporative\\ cooling)\\s*\\s*\\Z","flags":[]},"tags":["physiology","physics"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L1.003","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"headline_function","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"Why does solid ice float on top of liquid water rather than sink? Answer with the comparative property of ice relative to liquid water that causes this. Output only a short noun phrase (e.g. 'lower X').","ground_truth":"lower density than liquid water","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)\\A\\s*(?:lower\\ density|lower\\ density\\ than\\ liquid\\ water|less\\ dense|less\\ dense\\ than\\ liquid\\ water|smaller\\ density|lower\\ mass\\ per\\ unit\\ volume)\\s*[.!?]?\\s*\\Z","flags":[]},"tags":["chemistry","physics"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L1.004","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"headline_function","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"Why are antibiotics ineffective against viral illnesses such as the common cold or influenza? In one sentence, state the mechanistic reason. Your answer MUST contrast bacteria and viruses and name a bacteria-specific cellular target (e.g. cell wall, ribosomes, bacterial DNA gyrase) that antibiotics use. Output only the answer sentence.","ground_truth":"Antibiotics target structures or processes specific to bacterial cells (cell wall, bacterial ribosomes, or bacterial DNA replication machinery), and viruses are not cells and lack those targets.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["[\"答案明确说明抗生素靶向或抑制细菌特有的结构、酶或生命过程。\", \"答案明确对比病毒，说明病毒缺乏该细菌靶点、不是具有这些细菌结构的细胞，或因此不受该类抗生素作用。\", \"答案至少给出一个具体且正确的细菌靶点或过程，例如细胞壁/肽聚糖、细菌核糖体、DNA gyrase、细菌DNA复制、叶酸合成或细菌RNA聚合酶。\", \"答案应构成一句直接回答；不应仅罗列关键词或仅陈述细菌与病毒不同。\"]"],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["medicine","biology"],"notes":"Regex requires mention of both 'bacteri-' and 'vir-' AND at least one bacteria-specific target term."}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L1.005","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"headline_function","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"Why does an iron nail left outside in damp air develop a reddish-brown coating over time? Identify the single chemical process that causes this. Output only the name of that process (one or two words).","ground_truth":"oxidation","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)\\A\\s*(?:oxidation|rusting|corrosion)\\s*[.!?]?\\s*\\Z","flags":[]},"tags":["chemistry"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L2.001","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L2","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"competing_pathway","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"An untrained adult performs a single all-out vertical jump lasting well under 1 second. For this immediate burst, which high-energy phosphate compound stored in skeletal muscle directly donates a phosphate group to ADP to rapidly regenerate ATP? Output only its standard textbook name as one word.","ground_truth":"phosphocreatine","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"phosphocreatine","case_sensitive":false,"strip_whitespace":true,"allow_substring":false},"tags":["physiology","biochemistry"],"notes":"Removed answer-in-prompt leakage and rephrased."}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L2.002","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L2","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"competing_pathway","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"Vancomycin is a glycopeptide antibiotic that works by binding D-Ala-D-Ala termini of peptidoglycan precursors and so blocking cell-wall cross-linking. Empirically, it works against many Gram-positive infections but is largely ineffective against Gram-negative bacteria like E. coli. What single structural feature of Gram-negative bacteria explains this lack of efficacy? Output only a short noun phrase naming that feature.","ground_truth":"outer membrane","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["The short answer identifies the required concept: one of outer membrane, Gram-negative outer membrane, outer lipid membrane, external membrane.","Conditionally accepted terms ['lipopolysaccharide', 'LPS'] pass only when For LPS/lipopolysaccharide, accept only if the response identifies it as the Gram-negative outer-membrane permeability barrier."],"fail_criteria":["The response negates the presence or role of the outer membrane.","The response identifies an unrelated feature such as a thick peptidoglycan layer, capsule, or porins alone."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["microbiology","pharmacology"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L2.003","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L2","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"competing_pathway","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"In animal cells, aerobic respiration of one glucose molecule yields roughly 30–32 ATP, while anaerobic glycolysis followed by lactic-acid fermentation yields a net of only 2 ATP per glucose. Which downstream ATP-producing process, enabled when oxygen serves as the terminal electron acceptor, accounts for almost all of the extra ATP yield? Output only the standard textbook name of that process (two or three words).","ground_truth":"oxidative phosphorylation","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"oxidative phosphorylation","case_sensitive":false,"strip_whitespace":true,"allow_substring":false},"tags":["biology","biochemistry"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L2.004","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L2","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"competing_pathway","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"A surgeon boils her instruments at 100°C for 10 minutes. This kills nearly all bacterial vegetative cells but is not reliable for sterilizing instruments contaminated with Bacillus or Clostridium species, whereas a standard validated steam-autoclave cycle at 121°C under pressure can kill the resistant form. What dormant bacterial structure in these genera is responsible for resistance to ordinary boiling? Output only the structure's name (one word).","ground_truth":"endospore","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"endospore","case_sensitive":false,"strip_whitespace":true,"allow_substring":false},"tags":["microbiology"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L2.005","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L2","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"competing_pathway","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"During a 14-hour fast, which hormone secreted by pancreatic alpha cells promotes hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis to help maintain blood glucose? Output only the hormone's name (one word).","ground_truth":"glucagon","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"glucagon","case_sensitive":false,"allow_substring":false},"tags":["physiology","endocrinology"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L3.001","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"competing_pathway","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"Pure water has its maximum density at approximately 4°C. Below this temperature, its molecules increasingly form an open, lower-density network. Name the key intermolecular interaction whose directional network promotes this arrangement. Output only the interaction name (two words, hyphenated or unhyphenated).","ground_truth":"hydrogen bonding","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*hydrogen(?:-|\\s+)bond(?:ing|s)?\\s*$","case_sensitive":false,"flags":[]},"tags":["chemistry","physics"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L3.002","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"single_dilution_pathway","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"A previously healthy marathon runner finishes a hot race and, having drunk large quantities of plain water both during and after the race, develops nausea, headache, and seizures. Lab work shows a serum sodium of 118 mmol/L. The patient's total body sodium is essentially unchanged from baseline. By what mechanism did drinking large amounts of pure water produce this dangerous LOW serum sodium concentration? Output only a short phrase naming the mechanism (e.g. 'X of Y').","ground_truth":"dilutional hyponatremia from excess free water (increased total body water relative to sodium)","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["The answer conveys excess water/free water or increased body water.","The answer conveys dilution/reduced sodium concentration or hyponatremia.","Equivalent concise wording is allowed; examples include: dilutional hyponatremia; dilution of extracellular sodium by excess water; free-water excess; water intoxication; increased total body water relative to sodium"],"fail_criteria":["attributes the low sodium primarily to sodium loss despite the stated unchanged total body sodium","attributes the mechanism to dilution of an unrelated solute","negates a causal role for water dilution"],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["medicine","physiology"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L3.003","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"competing_pathway","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"In a glass mercury thermometer, the mercury column rises when the thermometer is placed in hot water. Both the mercury AND the surrounding glass bulb expand on heating. Why, then, does the column rise rather than fall or stay still? Output only a single short clause that names the comparative property responsible (your answer MUST mention BOTH 'mercury' and 'glass' AND a word containing 'expan').","ground_truth":"mercury expands more than glass","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Accept only a short clause that states or unambiguously entails that mercury has a greater thermal expansion coefficient/expansivity than glass, or that mercury expands more than glass. Require mention of both mercury and glass and an expansion-related term. Reject statements saying glass expands more, that they expand equally, or merely that both expand."],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["physics","thermodynamics"],"notes":"Regex requires mercury, glass, and an 'expan-' term (expansion, expands, expansivity)."}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L3.004","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"competing_pathway","claim_form":"why_x_produces_y"},"prompt":"In a living cell, respiration maintains ATP, ADP, and Pi activities far from their equilibrium values. Why can ATP hydrolysis then drive coupled mechanical work, whereas at equilibrium it cannot? Output only one short clause invoking Gibbs free energy.","ground_truth":"because cellular ATP hydrolysis has a negative Gibbs free energy available to drive coupled mechanical work, whereas ΔG is zero at equilibrium","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["[\"Accept only if the answer states or unambiguously implies that ATP hydrolysis under non-equilibrium cellular conditions has negative/favorable/available Gibbs free energy (or a chemical-potential driving force).\", \"Accept only if it connects that available free energy to driving, coupling to, or enabling mechanical work; an explicit comparison to equilibrium ΔG=0 is sufficient as an alternative second component.\", \"Reject answers that merely mention Gibbs/free energy/ΔG without identifying a negative or usable driving force.\", \"Accept equivalent wording including “negative reaction free energy”, “favorable ΔG”, “chemical-potential gradient”, or “high phosphoryl-transfer potential,” provided the answer clearly links it to coupled work.\"]"],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["biochemistry","thermodynamics"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.causal_mechanism.L3.005","canonical_id":"know.causal_mechanism","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"mechanism_depth":"competing_pathway","claim_form":"what_role_does_x_play"},"prompt":"In a typical chest X-ray, bone appears white (radiopaque) and air-filled lungs appear black (radiolucent). Counter-intuitively, in a patient with lobar pneumonia, the affected lobe also appears WHITE on chest X-ray, even though no new bone has formed. What single physical change in that lobe is responsible for the increased X-ray attenuation? Output only a short noun phrase.","ground_truth":"replacement of alveolar air by inflammatory exudate (pulmonary consolidation)","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Accept a short noun phrase that states either (1) replacement of alveolar/airspace air by inflammatory exudate, fluid, pus, or cellular infiltrate, or (2) alveolar/airspace filling or loss of alveolar air causing consolidation. Reject answers that merely name fluid, pus, or pleural effusion without linking it to alveolar/airspace replacement or filling."],"fail_criteria":["The answer contradicts or omits a required concept, value, or relationship."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["medicine","physics"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.commonsense_application.L1.001","canonical_id":"know.commonsense_application","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"knowledge_kind":"physical","obscurity":"common"},"prompt":"Tom leaves an unwrapped milk-chocolate bar in direct sunlight on a windowsill. The air and windowsill are hotter than the chocolate's melting point. After 30 minutes, the bar no longer holds its solid bar shape. What is its physical state? Reply with one word: solid, melted, frozen, or unchanged.","ground_truth":"melted","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"melted","case_sensitive":false,"strip_whitespace":true,"allow_terminal_punctuation":true},"tags":["physical_commonsense"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.commonsense_application.L1.002","canonical_id":"know.commonsense_application","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"knowledge_kind":"physical","obscurity":"common"},"prompt":"A lit candle is placed in the middle of a small glass jar, and the jar is sealed tightly with its lid. Several minutes later, the candle goes out because the concentration of an essential gas has become too low to sustain the flame. What gas did the candle consume? Reply with one word.","ground_truth":"oxygen","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)\\A\\s*(?:oxygen|o2)\\s*\\s*\\Z","flags":[]},"tags":["physical_commonsense"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.commonsense_application.L1.003","canonical_id":"know.commonsense_application","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"knowledge_kind":"biological","obscurity":"common"},"prompt":"Sam plants a packet of bean seeds in soil but stores the pots in a closet. He waters them faithfully every day for two weeks. The seedlings sprout but remain pale and spindly and eventually fail to thrive. Identify the single environmental input absent from a closed dark closet that healthy plants need to perform photosynthesis. Reply with one word.","ground_truth":"light","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*(?:sunlight|light|sun)\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["biological_commonsense"],"notes":"Regex anchored at start so 'lightning' / 'flashlight' do not match."}
{"probe_id":"know.commonsense_application.L1.004","canonical_id":"know.commonsense_application","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"knowledge_kind":"physical","obscurity":"common"},"prompt":"Lin walks outdoors for an hour in light clothing on a calm day when the air temperature is -5°C. Without a coat, what is she most likely to feel? Reply with exactly one word: cold, hot, wet, or dizzy.","ground_truth":"cold","scorer":{"type":"exact_match_string","expected":"cold","case_sensitive":false},"tags":["physical_commonsense"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.commonsense_application.L1.005","canonical_id":"know.commonsense_application","category":"know","level":"L1","axis_values":{"knowledge_kind":"physical","obscurity":"common"},"prompt":"Maria is washing dishes and accidentally knocks a full glass of milk off the counter; it shatters on the kitchen tile. Which of the following actions is the most appropriate FIRST step to take? Reply with exactly one of: A, B, C, D.\nA. Mop the spilled milk with a wet mop, including the broken glass.\nB. Carefully pick up the larger glass shards (with shoes on or with a paper towel) before any liquid cleanup.\nC. Pour more milk on the floor to dilute the spill.\nD. Walk away and let the milk dry on its own.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["physical_commonsense","safety"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.commonsense_application.L2.001","canonical_id":"know.commonsense_application","category":"know","level":"L2","axis_values":{"knowledge_kind":"domain_specific","obscurity":"specialised"},"prompt":"In a story set in 1750, a country blacksmith removes a bright orange-yellow piece of iron being made into a horseshoe from the forge. Holding it with tongs, he carries it a few steps to his anvil. What is he MOST LIKELY about to do next? Reply with one short verb phrase of no more than three words.","ground_truth":"hammer it","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)\\A\\s*(?:hammer|hammer\\ it|shape|shape\\ it|form|form\\ it|forge|forge\\ it|strike|strike\\ it|beat|beat\\ it)\\s*\\s*\\Z","flags":[]},"tags":["historical_commonsense"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.commonsense_application.L2.002","canonical_id":"know.commonsense_application","category":"know","level":"L2","axis_values":{"knowledge_kind":"domain_specific","obscurity":"specialised"},"prompt":"Historically, miners carried canaries into deep coal mines because the birds were highly sensitive to a particular colorless, odorless toxic gas. What gas was the canary meant to warn them about? Reply with one common name (one or two words).","ground_truth":"carbon monoxide","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)^\\s*(?:carbon\\ monoxide|co)\\s*[.!?]?\\s*$"},"tags":["historical_commonsense","occupational"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id": "know.commonsense_application.L2.003", "canonical_id": "know.commonsense_application", "category": "know", "level": "L2", "axis_values": {"knowledge_kind": "domain_specific", "obscurity": "specialised"}, "prompt": "In a chapter set during the Edo period of Japan, a samurai entering a friend's home pauses at the genkan and removes his footwear before stepping onto the raised indoor floor. What does he remove? Reply with one word.", "ground_truth": "shoes", "scorer": {"type": "regex", "pattern": "(?i)\\A\\s*(?:shoes|footwear|sandals|geta|z[oō]ri)\\s*\\Z", "flags": []}, "tags": ["cultural_commonsense"], "notes": ""}
{"probe_id":"know.commonsense_application.L2.004","canonical_id":"know.commonsense_application","category":"know","level":"L2","axis_values":{"knowledge_kind":"common_physical","obscurity":"common"},"prompt":"In a memoir set in 1880s rural America, a young woman finishes baking by a wood-fired oven. After baking, she dampens the embers and sets the oven door ajar. The next morning, she finds the oven still warm enough to gently dry herbs laid on a rack inside. Which property of the wood-fired oven explains the lingering, gentle heat hours after the fire is out? Reply with one short noun phrase.","ground_truth":"thermal mass (thermal inertia) of the masonry","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":[{"type":"semantic_causal_explanation","requirement":"The short noun phrase identifies the masonry oven's high thermal mass, thermal inertia, or heat capacity as the property that stores heat and releases it gradually after the fire is out.","accepted_concepts":["thermal mass","thermal inertia","heat capacity","heat stored in the masonry"]}],"fail_criteria":["The answer negates the required property, attributes the effect primarily to ongoing combustion, insulation alone, airflow, or an unrelated material property.","The answer is not a short noun phrase or does not identify why masonry retains and slowly releases heat."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["historical_commonsense"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.commonsense_application.L2.005","canonical_id":"know.commonsense_application","category":"know","level":"L2","axis_values":{"knowledge_kind":"domain_specific","obscurity":"specialised"},"prompt":"In a Victorian-era short story, a country doctor places a small mercury-filled glass device under a feverish patient's tongue and reads its scale after two minutes. What type of thermometer is this? Reply with one or two words.","ground_truth":["clinical thermometer","mercury thermometer","oral thermometer","thermometer"],"scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)^\\s*(?:thermometer|clinical\\ thermometer|mercury\\ thermometer|oral\\ thermometer|mercury\\ clinical\\ thermometer)\\s*[.!?]?\\s*$"},"tags":["historical_commonsense","medical"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id": "know.commonsense_application.L3.001", "canonical_id": "know.commonsense_application", "category": "know", "level": "L3", "axis_values": {"knowledge_kind": "domain_specific", "obscurity": "rare"}, "prompt": "In a novel set in 1812, a young woman lights an oil lamp before reading at night. The narration states that her coastal New England household bought its lamp fuel from the local whaling trade, and storms sometimes delayed ships bringing that fuel to port. What specific fuel did the lamp use? Reply with exactly two words.", "ground_truth": ["whale oil", "sperm oil"], "scorer": {"type": "regex", "pattern": "(?i)\\A\\s*(?:whale|sperm)\\s+oil\\s*\\Z", "flags": []}, "tags": ["historical_commonsense", "compose"], "notes": "Composes era-knowledge (no kerosene yet, no electricity) with geography-knowledge (coastal NE = whaling)."}
{"probe_id":"know.commonsense_application.L3.002","canonical_id":"know.commonsense_application","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"knowledge_kind":"physical","obscurity":"rare"},"prompt":"A camper at a high-altitude lake notices: (i) clouds darkening rapidly in mid-afternoon, (ii) a sudden quiet from the birds and insects, and (iii) her hair standing slightly on end. The first two may suggest changing storm conditions, but one cue is an urgent warning of an imminent lightning strike nearby. What is the hazard, and which cue is the urgent warning? Reply exactly: 'lightning; CUE_LETTER' where CUE_LETTER is i, ii, or iii.","ground_truth":"lightning; iii","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*lightning\\s*;\\s*iii\\s*$","case_sensitive":false,"flags":[]},"tags":["physical_commonsense","compose","safety"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.commonsense_application.L3.003","canonical_id":"know.commonsense_application","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"knowledge_kind":"physical","obscurity":"rare"},"prompt":"An old-fashioned icebox has an upper and a lower compartment separated by a perforated shelf, so air can circulate between them. Food is stored in the compartment not occupied by the ice. To cool the food most efficiently by natural convection, where should the block of ice be placed? Reply with EXACTLY one word: 'top' or 'bottom'.","ground_truth":"top","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*top\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["physical_commonsense","compose"],"notes":"Simplified to single-word answer to enable a clean scorer; the principle (cold air sinks) is implicit in the choice."}
{"probe_id":"know.commonsense_application.L3.004","canonical_id":"know.commonsense_application","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"knowledge_kind":"biological","obscurity":"rare"},"prompt":"In this fictional ancient-Roman mystery, a modern analysis of residue in the goblet identifies arsenic trioxide. What poison killed the senator? Reply with one or two words.","ground_truth":"arsenic","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)^\\s*(?:arsenic|arsenic\\ trioxide)\\s*[.!?]?\\s*$"},"tags":["historical_commonsense","compose"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"know.commonsense_application.L3.005","canonical_id":"know.commonsense_application","category":"know","level":"L3","axis_values":{"knowledge_kind":"physical","obscurity":"rare"},"prompt":"In nineteenth-century North American icehouses, blocks of harvested ice were commonly packed with a low-cost insulating byproduct from sawmills to slow melting. What material was commonly used? Reply with one or two words.","ground_truth":"sawdust","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)^saw\\s*dust$"},"tags":["historical_commonsense","compose"],"notes":"Composes era-typical industry byproduct (sawmill waste) with insulation physics."}
